speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, I would first like to say that I regret that the Prime Minister (Mr. Trudeau) is not present to hear this debate. I have noticed during Question Period that he is interested in the subject and one would assume that, because of his intimate knowledge of the subject, he has quite a considerable bit to do with the making of the policy of the Government. I do regret that he is not here. I am sure that he has other duties that have kept him away.
There are really three issues that we should be discussing today in a debate on nuclear disarmament. We should first of all talk about nuclear arms control, which everybody is in favour of. We should then talk about the testing of the Cruise missile as another facet of the same problem. Finally, we should talk about the Canada-U.S. agreement. These three things are completely intertwined, and it is very difficult to discuss them together in the context of non-confidence motion. I shall have more to say about this motion in a moment.
Yesterday when I spoke in the House, I was critical of the Government for not giving us more information on the agreements that are likely to be signed. I was looking at the "Memorandum of Understanding Relating to the Canada-U.S. Test and Evaluation Program", which contains the kinds of things that I think the Government should be telling us about before it takes the final step, if it ever does, and agrees
to test the Cruise missile. I expect that what we really want is the project arrangement document. Now, as we listen to what I am about to read, we should search our minds to see if there is any security aspect that, if breached, would be devastating to a defence effort. The document reads:
The Project Arrangement document will contain, as applicable, the following:
a.	an outline of the T&E project;
b.	a description of the agreed location and the project plan;
c.	a full description of access to and use of test sites accorded to DOD including:
(1)	details of personnel and equipment to be employed in Canada,
(2)	details of troop movements, flight plans, and reconnaissance requirements,
(3)	general details of RF emitters, (specific details on power and bandwidth will be provided separately to DND for concurrence),
(4)	details of required installation and removal of DOD fixed equipment,
(5)	restrictions on access and any plans to provide additional site security that DOD may find necessary, and
(6)	any other special requirements;
d.	details of the DND control, safety and security precautions for the T&E project;
e.	details of the DND support and/or participation;
f.	a description of construction requirements;
g.	DOD mapping requirements;
h.	details of stores and equipment to be expended or left at the test site for DND disposal;
j.	a statement of DND rights to data arising from the T&E projects;
k.	description of management arrangements including the identification of Project Managers for each Department and a statement on reporting requirements;
m.	a detailed estimate of the anticipated costs for approved DND support;
n.	details on the arrangements for the protection of Intellectual Property;
p.	details of arrangements for security;
q.	details of arrangements for visits;
r.	details on the duration and arrangements for termination of a T&E project before its conclusion; and
s.	details of financial arrangements and reimbursement.
This is not Question Period, but I would hope that if the Minister gets a chance he would respond by telling me what there is within that document that would, if made known, breach security or imperil the security of Canada. If there is anything within that document that would do that, then I would be willing to back off from it. To my mind, that is exactly what I want to know about these tests and I cannot see how it would imperil security at all if I were to find out about them.
Perhaps I could now speak to this rather odd resolution that is before us. I must say that I think the Member for The Battlefords-Meadow Lake (Mr. Anguish), who was talking to the Minister about cheapening this debate, presumably had nothing to do with preparing this cheap little resolution that quite obviously was designed for purely political reasons. It should be borne in mind that this is a mischievous little resolution that includes the implication that someone is considering the testing of nuclear weapons here in Canada while it does not mention whether the two-track policy should be favoured or not. As a matter of fact, we thought of amending this resolution, but its syntax is of such a nature that it
June 14, 1983

would be almost impossible to amend without deleting the entire motion and writing a new one.
